## Context

Part of the SOTA frontier-API experiment (umbrella 0166, synopsis §4).
Phase A of the experiment: each of the three SOTA agents is asked to
*design its own prompt and settings* to produce the best statistical
report it can under the budget cap. The three resulting prompts are
then used in Phase B (executed via the adapters from 0167–0169).

This ticket builds the harness for Phase A. It does **not** run the
queries — the user explicitly excluded query execution from this
raid.

## The meta-prompt

The Phase-A request to each agent is a single user message containing:

1. The *baseline prompt* — the existing `experiments/prompts/prompt_complete.txt`,
   verbatim, as the lower-bound starting point.
2. The *quality bar* — the four numbered subsections from `docs/synopsis.md`
   §2 ("Second, the quality bar"), verbatim. Use `extract_synopsis_section`
   (helper to add) so the prompt-design harness pulls the live text and
   does not drift from the paper.
3. The *task statement*: "Design the prompt and the settings you would
   use to produce the best statistical report on Vietnamese thermal
   power plants this method can yield, given a budget cap of $10 and
   an overnight wall-clock budget. Output: (a) the prompt body, (b) the
   recommended generation settings, (c) a brief rationale for the
   design. Your output will be executed unchanged."
4. A required structured-output envelope so the result is machine-
   parseable into `{prompt, settings, rationale}`.

## Actions

1. Create `experiments/sota/phase_a_prompt_design.py`:
   - Assembles the meta-prompt from the live synopsis + baseline.
   - Calls the configured adapter (0167/0168/0169) in dry-run.
   - When run with `--live`, sends one request per agent and writes
     the parsed `{prompt, settings, rationale}` plus raw transcript to
     `experiments/outputs/sota_phase_a/<agent>/<timestamp>.json`.
2. The harness must be agent-agnostic: it takes an adapter name and
   any adapter-specific options, then delegates. No agent-specific
   logic in this file.
3. Validate the structured-output envelope. If an agent ignores the
   schema, fail loudly with the raw payload so the human can re-issue
   the meta-prompt by hand.
4. Total Phase-A budget cap: $5 (covers 3 agents × ~$1 each plus
   retries).

## Test

`tests/test_phase_a_harness.py`:
- The assembled meta-prompt includes the verbatim baseline + verbatim
  quality-bar §2.
- The harness rejects responses missing the envelope keys.
- `--dry-run` against each registered adapter prints a fully formed
  request.
- Canned-response parsing produces a Phase-A record with non-empty
  `prompt`, `settings`, `rationale`.

## Exit criteria

- `--dry-run` works against all three adapters.
- Tests pass.
- Output directory layout documented in
  `experiments/outputs/sota_phase_a/README.md`.
- No live Phase-A calls have been made — those are an explicit, user-
  approved follow-up step.

## Notes

- The meta-prompt is the experiment. Treat it as a first-class artefact:
  freeze the synopsis revision SHA used to build it in each Phase-A
  record so the experiment is reproducible after the synopsis evolves.
- The output `prompt` from each agent becomes the input to Phase B
  (executed by re-invoking the same adapter with that prompt). Phase B
  is intentionally trivial harness given that 0167–0169 already provide
  single-call dispatch; no separate Phase-B ticket is required.
